```sql
-- Relational: normalized into tables, joined when needed
CREATE TABLE users (id INT PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(100));
CREATE TABLE orders (id INT PRIMARY KEY, user_id INT REFERENCES users(id), total DECIMAL(10,2));
SELECT u.name, o.total FROM users u JOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id;
```

```json
// NoSQL (MongoDB): the same user with orders embedded in one document
{ "_id": 1, "name": "Asha", "orders": [ { "total": 250.0 } ] }
```

Relational avoids duplication via joins; NoSQL trades duplication for fewer reads and easy horizontal scaling.

```javascript
// NoSQL misused: orders kept separate, then joined by hand in app code
const user = db.users.findOne({ _id: 1 });
const orders = db.orders.find({ userId: 1 }).toArray(); // extra round-trips
// Re-implementing joins in code throws away what a relational database does best.
```

```java
// Add dependency: spring-boot-starter-data-redis

@Service
public class ProductService {
    private final ProductRepository repository;

    @Cacheable(value = "products", key = "#id")
    public Optional<Product> findById(Long id) {
        // Expensive database query - Redis caches the result
        return repository.findById(id);
    }

    @CacheEvict(value = "products", key = "#product.id")
    public Product update(Product product) {
        return repository.save(product);
    }
}

@Configuration
@EnableCaching
public class RedisConfig {
    @Bean
    public RedisCacheManager cacheManager(RedisConnectionFactory factory) {
        return RedisCacheManager.builder(factory)
            .cacheDefaults(RedisCacheConfiguration.defaultCacheConfig()
                .entryTtl(Duration.ofMinutes(10)))  // Cache expires after 10 minutes
            .build();
    }
}
```

```sql
-- ACID: both rows commit together or not at all
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;  -- no reader ever sees a half-applied transfer

-- BASE (NoSQL): each write is accepted independently and reconciled later;
-- a replica may briefly return the old value (eventual consistency).
```

```sql
-- A crash between these two statements loses money
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
```

```sql
-- Both updates commit together, or neither does
START TRANSACTION;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance - 100 WHERE id = 1;
UPDATE accounts SET balance = balance + 100 WHERE id = 2;
COMMIT;  -- no reader ever sees a half-applied transfer
```

ACID is the right model when correctness cannot bend. A BASE store would let a replica briefly show the wrong balance.
